#!/bin/sh
# Git runs this file before every commit and stops the commit when it exits
# non-zero. Git looks for it here because `core.hooksPath` is `.githooks`, a
# setting that a clone does not copy: `just setup` sets it in a fresh clone with
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
# and afterwards `git config core.hooksPath` prints `.githooks`.
#
# On Windows, Git for Windows runs it with its own sh and recognises it as a
# script by the `#!/bin/sh` first line, so the executable bit does not matter
# there; git records the file as executable for Linux and macOS clones.
# Python always runs through uv (bare `python` is a Microsoft Store stub on
# this PC); `--no-project` keeps a commit from syncing `.venv` or relocking
# `uv.lock`.
#
# To run it by hand, in PowerShell or Git Bash, the way a commit runs it:
#     git hook run pre-commit
#
# It runs the data guard, `scripts/guard_data.py` (standard library only),
# which `just check` runs too. The commit stops when what it would contain
# holds market-data files outside fixtures/synthetic/, fixtures missing from
# their manifest, files over 25 MB, secrets, or notebooks with outputs
# (docs/masterplan/05-build-process.md section 5). This and the pre-push hook,
# which checks each commit a push sends, are the repository's only shell files
# (D-22, superseded in part by D-48).
exec uv run --no-project python scripts/guard_data.py
